  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Low pressure lifting through the region tonight\nthrough Sunday morning will produce showers and thunderstorms, \nas well as locally heavy rain resulting in a flash flood risk \nfor portions of the forecast area.\n\nHigh pressure over the area moves out to sea later today. Return\nflow sets up behind the high, and low level moisture will\nincrease over the area. Meanwhile, low pressure will organize\nand develop over the Mid-Atlantic. A warm front associated with\nthat low will lift north through the region this evening, and\nthen southerly flow will allow dew points to rise well into the\n60s and low 70s. PWATs will rise to 2-2.25 inches. \n\nLow pressure then follows north through the region late tonight\nthrough Sunday morning. This system will be fairly progressive,\nlifting from Delmarva around midnight tonight to Long Island by\nlate Sunday morning. However, due to the high moisture content \nin the atmosphere, there will be a 6-8 hour window during the \nearly morning hours on Sunday where as much as 1-2 inches of \nconvective rain will fall. The primary area of concern is the \nI-95 corridor and surrounding urban areas and small streams \nwhere flash flooding is more possible given any rapid runoff. \n\nPrecipitation tapers off Sunday morning as low pressure \ndeparts. A cold front swings through Sunday afternoon behind the\nlow, and then a secondary front may pass through the region \nSunday night. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...During the latter half of next week, a slow \nmoving cold front looks to make its way southward across the \narea bringing the potential for showers and storms. \n\nOnce we get through this weekend, attention will turn to \nThursday and Friday of next week. It should be first noted there\nis a lot of spread in the forecast guidance at this time given \nthis is about a week away. But overall, the signal in guidance \nis for a slow moving cold front to move southward through the \narea late next Thursday into Friday bring the potential for \nshowers and some storms. PWAT values along this front look to \npotentially be quite high (around 2 inches) but there remains \nsome questions about how much instability there will be as well \nas the speed and timing of the front as it moves through. The \nupshot of all this is that while forecast confidence is low, \nthis period will need to be monitored for heavy rain potential.",
